#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "105a5a4a-2fea-54bb-97c6-c2a700b05f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5548" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5aafdd7d-939a-5690-ac4d-46b554524d75" :question/math-academy-id "e-5549" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d279bb1d-754e-5044-9a01-d4c86f30b269" :question/math-academy-id "e-5550" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "35e7ce1a-b2cc-50dd-8bf7-24d20e55d623" :question/math-academy-id "e-5908" :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac47e7cf-caaa-5f81-81a8-c53bd757c1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308507} {:answer/id #uuid "df89db0b-eeef-5618-8765-65957c5d75b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308508} {:answer/id #uuid "e4758cb0-61fb-5fa9-88b1-6e7f6d2326dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308509} {:answer/id #uuid "8c41e0f4-705a-56bd-b39a-8abe336523f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308510} {:answer/id #uuid "12d336ee-0d17-540f-8180-c981b7c25631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4758cb0-61fb-5fa9-88b1-6e7f6d2326dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd76808-9a8b-58eb-9a64-8f4ac3af00d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cfd7234-83ad-57e9-960b-f838a9b42ca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141167" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=2,\\,p(2)=4,\\,p(3)=8.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba06921c-997b-516d-8291-540cf4b031aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308514} {:answer/id #uuid "380a9d83-dd4c-5f2e-beac-af7ddc86f847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308515} {:answer/id #uuid "826dff1e-eda2-541a-bfbc-fb1f4f6f09f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc2c4c9-5baf-5a56-8da9-d4b1cfbe8957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308517} {:answer/id #uuid "68795571-006f-58aa-ae3f-9ce848f8b9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826dff1e-eda2-541a-bfbc-fb1f4f6f09f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ceeabe5-4f09-5832-9388-27a782316d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0f46084-f755-54b3-bb66-e1e926747894" :question/math-academy-id "q-141170" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b143890-0503-5b24-8531-6e85acf23ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi +2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308521} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdb1a46-0d13-58b3-84ac-263992a26deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308522} {:answer/id #uuid "314dc21b-8871-5df2-a10f-d67e3bed48fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308523} {:answer/id #uuid "83c6089a-5085-5afa-9420-c8c743c14463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308524} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c66ed9-f5a7-5e46-b119-fe80c0508e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1c66ed9-f5a7-5e46-b119-fe80c0508e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308525} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0e9478-9f06-55a6-a86b-6a58136206e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a38372-c4e4-54ba-9d14-0563306ec42c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141171" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-((-\\pi )+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb729d0-97da-52b8-9d80-164e9e46a853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132393} {:answer/id #uuid "18a4a8c1-2d56-51db-a64c-2686de79d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132394} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad725d4-4e91-5b33-9afe-1b3839e539ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132395} {:answer/id #uuid "38e27199-ce70-587d-afbc-4d70e86e05dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132396} {:answer/id #uuid "4837fdcc-4024-5a9d-b1e5-a9caf935c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad725d4-4e91-5b33-9afe-1b3839e539ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132395} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f413739-335e-54d9-b370-a986cce8378a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27a47d93-091f-5b05-b6ac-49761283f337" :question/math-academy-id "q-42714" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+5{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad991df1-d65d-5dd0-993b-a41b1b0506a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132417} {:answer/id #uuid "00d82f81-e661-5ad5-83ea-b2fb5edbe2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132418} {:answer/id #uuid "0493056c-f245-55a1-9c75-5d1a97b1c15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{50}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132419} {:answer/id #uuid "518ec4a9-e4ea-5643-84b6-3ce33513d026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132420} {:answer/id #uuid "7e08f1cb-2b3c-5192-a8cd-e31ab51eebd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00d82f81-e661-5ad5-83ea-b2fb5edbe2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132418} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d7502c-5e95-5cb1-b7bc-542be514f2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "443381c1-3014-5c89-915a-d8f5ec0d3345" :question/math-academy-id "q-42741" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb74c47-bb4c-5e59-831b-f4db6f207238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308528} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd88ade-f649-591f-a667-bace87399085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308529} {:answer/id #uuid "7209ba9c-9602-5431-9c0f-4d49cc1732fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308530} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ee8927-137c-5f76-9b8a-6ae96460c881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308531} {:answer/id #uuid "a33baad8-9adb-510a-9d9f-94fe9cf0f431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a33baad8-9adb-510a-9d9f-94fe9cf0f431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a422833-c88d-5729-a704-8c93d9261cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e498321f-6c47-59d7-91ad-fafbad52af14" :question/math-academy-id "q-43908" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+7{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-2))+7\\cdot (0\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890dfec2-e35c-51f0-84e0-fb7923b16b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{103}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308535} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3741ae-1348-5340-95f0-a2bed477867b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536} {:answer/id #uuid "63053eb2-3740-5954-987e-c4e4e26f1bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308537} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3d9257-414a-59e1-9394-a8b362afbd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308538} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84eb3d-ef95-5085-89de-fd40f21602cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63053eb2-3740-5954-987e-c4e4e26f1bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308537} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3da0305-2f92-5dc2-bb82-4c3ad9ce07e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e5ddf58-998d-561f-b859-a4149f98cf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43920" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2t+\\sqrt{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+\\sqrt{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{8{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a002ae-5d18-5008-a449-3044be9442ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132585} {:answer/id #uuid "8c868fd5-30ae-5c97-9c38-67aa87ff3ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132586} {:answer/id #uuid "9c122015-f93d-5b7c-a06f-257c305e7f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132587} {:answer/id #uuid "118bf933-b441-5ca3-bfbb-bb0aaa67703d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132588} {:answer/id #uuid "75dc88ad-4214-5120-9f2c-6ad45ef89b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c868fd5-30ae-5c97-9c38-67aa87ff3ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7674dab-2393-5d58-9d08-9c46e561836e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a33cdb57-0054-565e-b0b2-5d6c1b3e919e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43925" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca2cfff-9996-5647-81bf-56d33d2fd79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308542} {:answer/id #uuid "092efdca-3c93-5998-ac53-82de3cf614cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308543} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0ecd9-1e11-514e-9d00-dabbaa932d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308544} {:answer/id #uuid "49cdd3a2-06c8-54df-9499-74e4574715a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308545} {:answer/id #uuid "57bc95a7-7876-5edd-918f-60b825ca10c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{t}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0ecd9-1e11-514e-9d00-dabbaa932d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308544} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5cbcd1-b3e0-5621-bd4b-2aa9e3877d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5cdc838-4f6b-5bb7-a174-36b0b232f02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43926" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=1-\\sqrt{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(1-2\\sqrt{t}+t),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t-\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\sqrt{t})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f94225-f841-503b-b628-9340e642f28a" 